= Comments on related philosophical and political questions

== Protect the objectivity of science

As argued above, "innate gender identity" is not sustainable at the levels of logic or philosophy of science, and is better treated as a cultural concept or ideology.

From a utilitarian perspective, "born this way" narratives may increase inclusion of transgender people @Glazier_2021, just as religion can increase trust, reciprocity, volunteering, social support, and wellbeing @Aksoy_2023@Shariff_2016@Chen_2020. (This is a functional analogy. I am not saying "born this way is a religion.") However, scientists do not study souls or heaven because religion has social utility. Therefore, social and political utility does not justify importing "born this way" narrative into scientific practice. It belongs to cultural and political discourse.

Scientists should protect the objectivity and conceptual rigour of science. We should not accommodate either conservative binary gender essentialism or progressive transgender essentialism. These positions differ morally but are both ideological in epistemic structure. History repeatedly shows the risks of ideological invasion in natural science.

== Conversion therapy

This model may invite discussion of conversion therapy. If gender identity is shaped by social construct, does that imply it can be modified or recoded?

Yes, I acknowledge it. There is no current evidence for a natural law that makes it impossible to change one's gender identity in principle.

While this does not mean conversion therapy is effective, it is a methodological error. Predictive processing operates largely unconsciously and can feel innate. It is not a voluntary choice and cannot simply be renounced by decision.

More importantly, individuals attach subjective meaning to their identities and often consciously resist attempts to "convert" them.
For analogy, I am a physicalist, Enlightenment rationalist, scientific realist, and Kantian (only the normative ethics, not the transcendental idealism). If I were sent to an illegal institution and subjected to some methods to "convert" me into an idealist, post-structuralist, scientific anti-realist, or utilitarian, the attempt would definitely fail. This is not because those positions are innate. No serious scholar claims one "is born an Enlightenment rationalist". It would fail because these commitments are part of my core self-identity and normatively significant to me, and I would resist coercive change.

History offers many analogous cases. One may cite Socrates, early Christian martyrs, Husayn ibn Ali and his followers, loyalist Confucian officials in dynastic transitions, French revolutionaries, and socialist or communist revolutionaries. A person's willingness to die rather than renounce a conviction does neither require innateness, nor prove it.

Conversion therapy is unethical not because it violates a presumed "natural state", which would be a naturalistic fallacy. This is structurally isomorphic to conservative attacks on transgender people, with "innate binary gender" replaced by "innate gender identity." Genetic diseases are innate, therefore, they should be respected according to this rule. This conclusion that is clearly absurd.

Conversion therapy is unethical because it violates rational agency and autonomy, and treats persons as means rather than ends @Kant_2011.
If we universalise it as a maxim, it could be: "Whenever I judge another person's gender identity to violate my preferred norm, I may attempt to change it, and this permission applies to all rational agents." I admit that there is no formal contradiction. A society where everybody wants to reshape others' identity is logically possible. Yet a rational agent cannot will this as a universal law without undermining their own status as autonomous legislator. One cannot consistently will a norm that grants all agents authority to reconstruct other agents' identities by their judgment. This conclusion depends on neither predictive processing theory nor "born this way".